      {
        "id": "section_1",
        "narration": "You've probably heard that Docker containers are like lightweight virtual machines. That explanation is simple, popular, and fundamentally wrong. <break time='0.6s'/> A container doesn't boot a guest operating system, run a hypervisor, or virtualize hardware. It's just a standard Linux process running directly on your host CPU. Docker is simply tricking that process into believing it's alone on the machine. Once you understand how that trick works, container optimization becomes second nature.",
        "objective": "",
