        "subtitles": [
          {
            "text": "You've probably heard that Docker containers are like lightweight virtual machines.",
            "start": 0.1,
            "end": 5.05,
            "startMs": 100,
            "endMs": 5050,
            "type": "sentence"
          },
          {
            "text": "That explanation is simple, popular, and fundamentally wrong.",
            "start": 5,
            "end": 9.688,
            "startMs": 5000,
            "endMs": 9688,
            "type": "sentence"
          },
          {
            "text": "A container doesn't boot a guest operating system, run a hypervisor, or virtualize hardware.",
            "start": 9.688,
            "end": 16.05,
            "startMs": 9688,
            "endMs": 16050,
            "type": "sentence"
          },
          {
            "text": "It's just a standard Linux process running directly on your host CPU.",
            "start": 16.05,
            "end": 20.913,
            "startMs": 16050,
            "endMs": 20912,
            "type": "sentence"
          },
          {
            "text": "Docker is simply tricking that process into believing it's alone on the machine.",
            "start": 20.913,
            "end": 25.638,
            "startMs": 20912,
            "endMs": 25638,
            "type": "sentence"
          },
          {
            "text": "Once you understand how that trick works, container optimization becomes second nature.",
            "start": 25.637,
            "end": 31.362,
            "startMs": 25638,
            "endMs": 31362,
            "type": "sentence"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "section_2",
        "narration": "So how does Docker pull off this illusion? Through two core Linux kernel features: Namespaces and Control Groups. Namespaces restrict what a process can see. A PID namespace gives the process its own fake tree where it thinks it's PID 1. A Net namespace isolates network interfaces, and a Mount namespace hides the host filesystem. Control Groups, or cgroups, restrict what the process can use — capping memory, CPU shares, and I/O. <break time='0.6s'/> That's why spinning up a container takes milliseconds instead of minutes. When you run docker run, you're launching a host process wrapped in kernel security boundaries. There is no guest kernel overhead, no wasted RAM, and zero hypervisor translation.",
        "objective": "",
