      {
        "id": "section_3",
        "narration": "The second major piece of Docker magic is the Union File System. Container images aren't big monolithic disk images — they are stacks of immutable, read-only layers. When you run a container, Docker slaps a paper-thin, writable layer right on top using copy-on-write logic. But here's where most developers ruin their build times: every single directive in a Dockerfile creates a layer that gets cached. <break time='0.6s'/> If you copy your entire source tree before running your package manager, every tiny code change invalidates the cache for all subsequent steps. By copying just package.json first, running install, and then copying your application code, Docker reuses cached layers instantly. That tiny order flip turns five-minute CI/CD builds into five seconds.",
        "objective": "",
        "visuals": [
