      {
        "id": "section_2_v1",
        "type": "ArchitectureScene",
        "title": "Kernel Isolation",
        "subtitle": "Namespaces + cgroups",
        "narration": "So how does Docker pull off this illusion? Through two core Linux kernel features: Namespaces and Control Groups. Namespaces restrict what a process can see. A PID namespace gives the process its own fake tree where it thinks it's PID 1. A Net namespace isolates network interfaces, and a Mount namespace hides the host filesystem. Control Groups, or cgroups, restrict what the process can use — capping memory, CPU shares, and I/O. <break time='0.6s'/> That's why spinning up a container takes milliseconds instead of minutes. When you run docker run, you're launching a host process wrapped in kernel security boundaries. There is no guest kernel overhead, no wasted RAM, and zero hypervisor translation.",
        "actualDurationSec": 0,
        "estimatedDurationSec": 0,
